---
// What authors say their labels are, and are not, relative to each other.
//
// Taxonomy from claims rather than from a committee. `distinguishes-from` and
// `interprets` are the common relations, not the permitted ones: `relation` is an
// open string, so an author who needs a word this schema never anticipated writes
// it rather than asking for it.
//
// This is how disagreement becomes legible instead of merely present. Two people
// claiming `kindness` is a fact you can read off a label page. One of them saying
// "this is not what I mean, and here is why" is the content.
interface Props {
  relations: any[];
  label: string;
}
const { relations, label } = Astro.props;
---

{relations.length === 0 ? (
  <p class="note absent-block">
    Nobody claiming <strong>{label}</strong> has said how their reading relates to
    anyone else's. The claims sit side by side without any author addressing the
    others, so a reader has to infer the disagreement from the definitions.
  </p>
) : (
  relations.map((r) => (
    <div class="relation">
      <div class="provenance">
        <a class="mono" href={`/${r.from_author}/`}>{r.from_author}</a>
        {" "}<span class="rel-verb">{r.relation.replace(/-/g, " ")}</span>{" "}
        <a class="mono" href={`/${r.to_author}/`}>{r.to_author}</a>
        <span class="mono">/{r.to_label}</span>
      </div>
      {r.note && <p class="definition">{r.note}</p>}
    </div>
  ))
)}

{relations.length > 0 && (
  <p class="note">
    These are claims, not categories. Nobody is filed under anything here: an
    author states how they read their own label against someone else's, and the
    other author is free to disagree in the same way. The registry records both and
    settles neither.
  </p>
)}
